##TECHNICAL DOCUMENTATION
AI Presentation Builder
Technical Overview and System Documentation
Item	Details
Frontend	React, Vite, Tailwind CSS
Backend	Java 21, Spring Boot
Database	MySQL
AI	Groq API with LLaMA model
Document Processing	Apache PDFBox and Apache POI
Main Purpose	Generate structured presentations from text or documents

 
1. Introduction
AI Presentation Builder is a web application that uses artificial intelligence to automatically create the structure and content of a presentation. A user can either enter a topic or upload a document. The system processes the input and generates a presentation containing slides, bullet points, speaker notes, visual suggestions and possible audience questions.
The project is built as a full-stack application. React handles the user interface, Spring Boot handles the backend logic, MySQL stores generated presentations, and the Groq API provides the language model.


2. Problem Statement
Creating a presentation manually takes time because the user has to organize information, decide what goes on each slide, write concise points and prepare speaker notes. The project aims to automate these repetitive tasks while still giving the user a structured presentation that can be reviewed and modified.


3. Objectives
•	Generate presentations from a topic or text.
•	Generate presentations from uploaded PDF, DOCX, TXT and Markdown files.
•	Use an LLM to organize information into meaningful slides.
•	Generate speaker notes and visual suggestions.
•	Store generated presentations for later viewing.
•	Provide a simple web interface for generation and history.


4. System Architecture
The application follows a simple client-server architecture. The React frontend communicates with the Spring Boot backend through REST APIs. The backend communicates with the AI service and database.
Component	Technology	Role
Frontend	React + Vite	User interface and presentation display
Backend	Spring Boot	API, business logic and processing
AI Service	Groq + LLaMA	Generates presentation content
Database	MySQL	Stores generated presentations
Document Parser	PDFBox + Apache POI	Extracts text from uploaded files

4.1 Basic Architecture Flow
User → React Frontend → Spring Boot Backend → Groq AI → Generated JSON → MySQL → React Frontend
For document input, the flow becomes: User → React → Spring Boot → Document Text Extraction → Groq AI → Generated Presentation → MySQL → React.


5. End-to-End Workflow
1.	The user enters a topic or selects a document.
2.	The React frontend sends the information to the backend.
3.	The backend validates the input.
4.	If a document is uploaded, its text is extracted.
5.	The extracted text or user topic is sent to the Groq API.
6.	The LLaMA model generates structured presentation content.
7.	The backend checks and converts the response into application objects.
8.	The presentation is saved in MySQL.
9.	The backend sends the result to React.
10.	React displays the generated presentation.


6. Frontend
The frontend is responsible for everything the user sees and interacts with. It is built using React and Vite, with Tailwind CSS used for styling.

6.1 Main Frontend Components
Component	Purpose
App.jsx	Controls the main application and overall state
GenerateForm.jsx	Accepts text and document uploads
DeckView.jsx	Displays the generated presentation
SlideCard.jsx	Displays an individual slide
PresentationList.jsx	Displays previously generated presentations
Spinner.jsx	Shows loading status
api.js	Handles communication with the backend

6.2 Frontend State
React state is used to keep track of the user's input, selected file, loading status, errors, current presentation and presentation history. useEffect is used to load previous presentations when the application starts.


7. Backend
The backend is developed using Java 21 and Spring Boot. It acts as the central part of the application. It receives requests from React, processes the input, communicates with the AI service, stores the result and sends a response back to the frontend.

7.1 Backend Layers
Layer	Purpose
Controller	Receives HTTP requests and returns responses
Service	Contains the main application logic
Repository	Communicates with MySQL
DTO	Defines the structure of request and response data
Exception	Handles application errors
Configuration	Handles settings such as CORS


8. AI / LLM Integration
The project uses the Groq API to access a LLaMA language model. The backend sends the user's topic or document text to the model along with instructions describing how the presentation should be generated.

8.1 Why Groq?
•	Provides access to high-performance LLM inference.
•	Offers an API that can be called from the backend.
•	Allows the model to be changed through configuration.
•	Suitable for applications where response speed is important.

8.2 Why LLaMA?
LLaMA is used as the language model responsible for understanding the user's topic and generating the presentation content. The model is not responsible for the web application, database or file processing; it only performs the language-generation part.

8.3 Prompt Engineering
The backend sends a system prompt that tells the model exactly what to generate. The prompt asks for a fixed JSON structure instead of normal paragraphs. This makes the AI response easier for the Java backend and React frontend to understand.
The generated presentation contains:
•	Presentation title
•	Five to eight slides
•	Slide headings
•	Three to five bullet points per slide
•	Speaker notes
•	Visual recommendations
•	Likely audience questions


9. Document Processing
The application supports four document types. The backend extracts text before sending it to the AI model.
File Type	Technology Used	Purpose
PDF	Apache PDFBox	Extracts text from PDF files
DOCX	Apache POI	Extracts text from Word documents
TXT	Java UTF-8	Reads plain text
MD	Java UTF-8	Reads Markdown text

The extracted text is checked before generation. The current system limits the extracted content to 20,000 characters to avoid excessively large requests to the LLM.


10. REST API
The frontend communicates with the backend through four main endpoints.
Method	Endpoint	Purpose
POST	/api/presentations/generate	Generate a presentation from text
POST	/api/presentations/generate/upload	Generate a presentation from a file
GET	/api/presentations	Get presentation history
GET	/api/presentations/{id}	Get one saved presentation

11. Error Handling
The backend has a global exception handler so that errors are returned in a consistent format. Common cases include:
Problem	Status
Empty or invalid text	400 Bad Request
Unsupported file	400 Bad Request
File too large	400 Bad Request
Presentation not found	404 Not Found
AI/Groq failure	502 Bad Gateway
Unexpected server error	500 Internal Server Error

12. Security
The Groq API key is stored on the backend and is loaded through environment configuration. It is not placed inside the React frontend. This prevents users from directly seeing the secret through frontend source code.
Important security considerations for a production version include:
•	Add user authentication.
•	Restrict access to presentations by user.
•	Use a secure secret-management system.
•	Add rate limiting.
•	Validate uploaded files more strictly.
•	Scan uploaded files for malicious content.
•	Add protection against prompt injection in uploaded documents.


13. Configuration
The application uses environment variables so that sensitive information and deployment-specific settings do not need to be hard-coded.
Configuration	Purpose
GROQ_API_KEY	Groq authentication key
GROQ_MODEL	LLM model to use
GROQ_BASE_URL	Groq API endpoint
DB_URL	MySQL connection URL
DB_USERNAME	Database username
DB_PASSWORD	Database password

14. Running the Project

14.1 Start Backend
cd backend
./mvnw spring-boot:run
The backend runs on port 8080 by default.

14.2 Start Frontend
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
The frontend runs through the Vite development server, normally on port 5173.


15. Current Limitations
•	The application does not currently export a PowerPoint file.
•	The generated presentation is displayed as a web-based deck.
•	AI-generated information is not independently fact-checked.
•	There is no authentication.
•	There is no multi-user presentation ownership.
•	Automated test coverage is currently limited.


